# Specialization

**Specialization** is the narrowing of a general principle, under added
constraints, to a more specific rule or application. It is the inverse of
[[generalization]]. The discussion gives it one table row; what follows places
it among its neighbours.

## Description

| | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|
| Specialization | general principle and constraints | more specific rule or application |

The input has two parts, and the second is what distinguishes the operation.
A principle alone specializes to nothing in particular. The constraints say
which narrower case is wanted.

**Close to deduction, and different.** Applying a general principle to a case
looks like [[deduction]], and when the principle is a formal statement and the
constraint a substitution, it is one: an instance of a theorem follows from
the theorem. The operations come apart elsewhere. Deduction derives
consequences and keeps the statement's generality where it can. Specialization
aims at a restricted formulation and gives generality up on purpose. And the
choice of constraints is not derived from anything: which special case is
worth stating is a judgement.

**Close to generation, and different.** Both run from the general to the
particular. Specialization returns a narrower rule. [[generation]] returns
instances.

The discussion specializes twice in its main line of argument.

- The **diagonal lemma** holds for every formula with one free variable.
  Choosing the formula "is not provable" specializes it to the
  [[godel-sentence]]. All the interest lies in the choice.
- The proposed *reflective formal system* is a wide class. The *Gödelian*
  subclass adds one requirement, a diagonal fixed point with a provable
  limitation, and so specializes it.

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: a principle and constraints in, a narrower rule out.
- Its inverse is [[generalization]] {relation: contrasts}.
- It is close to [[deduction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: an instance follows deductively, but the choice of constraints does not.
- It differs from [[generation]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a narrower rule, against instances.
- The [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of} is the diagonal lemma specialized to unprovability.
- The Gödelian subclass specializes the [[reflective-formal-system]] {relation: example-of}.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[generalization]].** Widening a concept or claim to cover more cases.
  Specialization recovers the old cases from the wider one.
- **[[deduction]].** Inference from premises and rules to what must follow.
  It supplies the step from principle to instance, without choosing the
  instance.
- **[[godel-sentence]].** A sentence equivalent to the statement of its own
  unprovability. It is one particular choice inside a lemma that holds for
  every formula.

